WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.230
Here’s the video explaining what’s wrong
with the two envelope fallacy.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.390
I read a lot of different creative solutions
from you guys.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:09.170
Here is one that is satisfying for me.

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:15.480
Again, we have 2 envelopes, and for concreteness
we’ll say one has $1 and the other two.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.050
I pick an envelop but don’t open it.

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:24.320
Remember the fallacy went like this: my envelope
has some unknown amount X, but the other envelope

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:30.429
has either 2X or 1/2 X.

00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:37.870
So in expectation, it has X plus a quarter
X, which is more than X.

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:39.900
So I should swap.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.250
Now here is my solution.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:45.820
Let’s think of it in terms of the actually
money assigned, and then it will be super

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:47.640
obvious what’s wrong.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:54.330
Say that, unknown to me, my envelope contains
$1, so X=$1.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:56.000
Then what does the other contain?

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.290
It’s $2, right?

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:03.690
But my previous calculation was trying to
say it’s either half a dollar or $2.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:12.750
And similarly, if my X is actually $2, then
the other is $1 dollar, never $4 dollars.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:19.560
So claiming that the other envelope contains
either 2X or half X is wrong.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:25.460
What that’s saying is, regardless of X this
is true about the other envelopes value.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:37.320
What’s true is that you don’t know if
X is 1 or 2 dollars.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:48.870
So 

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:58.030
the expectation value of the second envelope
is the probability your X is $1 times the

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:04.320
amount in the other envelope in that case
plus the probability your X is $2 plus the

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.590
amount in the other envelope in that case.

00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:10.060
And you put the numbers in, and you get $1.5.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.329
What you’d expect- half way between $1 and
$2.

00:02:11.329 --> 00:02:14.060
So you don’t bother swapping

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.000
Thank you for all your comments about this
fallacy.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:21.870
Lots of you saw what the problem was, but
what I also really really liked is that, even

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:25.670
when many of you couldn’t figure it out,
you still commented with some ideas you had

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:27.349
about it and some hunches.

00:02:27.349 --> 00:02:32.500
That’s the point of all these puzzle videos
I make- it’s to get you thinking.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.859
Whether you get the answer or not is irrelevant.

